menu "gpSP experiments"
    config GBSP_FAST_RAM_WRITES
        bool "Direct interpreter writes to IWRAM/EWRAM"
        default n
        help
            Experimental fast path for ordinary work RAM stores only.
            Keeps original cycle accounting and all non-RAM handlers.
            Ignored with dynarec/SMC. Compare on hardware before adopting.
    config GBSP_MEMORY_PROFILE
        bool "Count interpreter data accesses (diagnostic only)"
        default n
        help
            Count interpreter data reads/writes by region and width.
            Excludes instruction fetch, DMA, PPU and addresses >= 0x10000000.
            Writes window totals to gbsp-memory.txt. Adds hot-path overhead;
            do not compare its timings with normal builds. Ignored with dynarec.
    config GBSP_COMPACT_READS
        bool "Share range check in interpreter scalar reads"
        default n
        help
            Experimental scalar load layout. Preserves original handlers,
            signed loads and timing. Compare against mapped RAM stores alone.
    config GBSP_CPSR_CONSUMER_SYNC
        bool "Sync CPSR flags at the sites that read them"
        default n
        help
            Adds collapse_flags() to MSR and to BX ARM->Thumb, the only two
            sites that need it and do not already do it. Idempotent while the
            per-instruction sync is still in place, so this alone must not
            change behaviour. Enable it first, and confirm that, before
            enabling the lazy dispatch below. Ignored with dynarec.
    config GBSP_CPSR_LAZY_DISPATCH
        bool "Drop the per-instruction CPSR flag sync"
        default n
        select GBSP_CPSR_CONSUMER_SYNC
        help
            Removes collapse_flags() from the top of arm_loop/thumb_loop, so
            the flag cache in reg[CPSR] is refreshed only by the consumers.
            14 Xtensa instructions per interpreted instruction; whether that
            becomes speed is a hardware question. Ignored with dynarec.
    config GBSP_SPARSE_REGISTERS
        bool "Iterate selected registers in block transfers"
        default n
        help
            Experimental ARM/Thumb register-list iteration using ctz.
            Keeps transfer order, addresses, handlers and writeback unchanged.
    config GBSP_FETCH_PC
        bool "Reuse PC within an instruction fetch"
        default n
        help
            Experimental ARM/Thumb fetch layout. Preserves slow-path reload,
            mapping, opcode reads and cycles. Ignored with dynarec.
    config GBSP_NO_CHEAT_HOOK
        bool "Remove the per-instruction cheat-hook test (Candidato A, passo 2)"
        default n
        help
            Drops the cheat_master_hook snapshot and the PC==hook comparison
            from the top of arm_loop/thumb_loop entirely (2 Xtensa
            instructions per interpreted instruction on top of the snapshot
            already taken unconditionally). cheat_parse() then refuses every
            code with CheatErrorNotSupported instead of accepting one that
            can never fire. Turn this off again before wiring up any cheat
            UI. Host-diff verified; never measured on hardware in isolation.
    config GBSP_CPU_RODATA_DRAM
        bool "Place cpu.cpp read-only data (dispatch tables) in internal DRAM"
        default n
        help
            Maps the .rodata of cpu.cpp (the jump tables used by the ARM
            condition/opcode and Thumb opcode dispatch in execute_arm, plus
            bit_count, cpu_modes and PSR masks, ~3KB) to internal DRAM
            instead of flash-mapped rodata, which shares the data cache
            with PSRAM. Instructions are unchanged; only table addresses
            move. Costs internal DRAM. Never measured on hardware.
    config GBSP_RENDER_CORE1
        bool "Render scanlines on core 1 (experimental)"
        default n
        help
            Hands each visible scanline to a worker task pinned to core 1,
            below rg_display/rg_input, while core 0 keeps emulating. At most
            one job is pending; it renders from a copy of the video I/O
            registers, and every other writer of VRAM, OAM, palette or the
            renderer's own state waits for it first. Falls back to the
            synchronous path if the task cannot be created. Prototype: not
            measured on hardware.
    config GBSP_RENDER_CORE1_STATS
        bool "Count render worker jobs, waits and busy time"
        depends on GBSP_RENDER_CORE1
        default n
        help
            Adds a render_core1 line to the profile log. Diagnostic only.
endmenu
